    <p class="title is-5 mathjax">
      
        Reverse Engineering User Stories from Code using Large <span class="search-hit mathjax">Language</span> <span class="search-hit mathjax">Models</span>
      
    </p>
    <p class="authors">
      <span class="has-text-black-bis has-text-weight-semibold">Authors:</span>
      
      <a href="/search/?searchtype=author&amp;query=Ouf%2C+M">Mohamed Ouf</a>, 
      
      <a href="/search/?searchtype=author&amp;query=Li%2C+H">Haoyu Li</a>, 
      
      <a href="/search/?searchtype=author&amp;query=Zhang%2C+M">Michael Zhang</a>, 
      
      <a href="/search/?searchtype=author&amp;query=Guizani%2C+M">Mariam Guizani</a>
      
    </p>
    
    <p class="abstract mathjax">
      <span class="has-text-black-bis has-text-weight-semibold">Abstract</span>:
      <span class="abstract-short has-text-grey-dark mathjax" id="2509.19587v1-abstract-short" style="display: inline;">
        User stories are essential in agile development, yet often missing or outdated in legacy and poorly documented systems. We investigate whether large <span class="search-hit mathjax">language</span>&hellip;
        <a class="is-size-7" style="white-space: nowrap;" onclick="document.getElementById('2509.19587v1-abstract-full').style.display = 'inline'; document.getElementById('2509.19587v1-abstract-short').style.display = 'none';">&#9661; More</a>
      </span>
      <span class="abstract-full has-text-grey-dark mathjax" id="2509.19587v1-abstract-full" style="display: none;">
        User stories are essential in agile development, yet often missing or outdated in legacy and poorly documented systems. We investigate whether large <span class="search-hit mathjax">language</span> <span class="search-hit mathjax">models</span> (<span class="search-hit mathjax">LLMs</span>) can automatically recover user stories directly from source code and how prompt design impacts output quality. Using 1,750 annotated C++ snippets of varying complexity, we evaluate five state-of-the-art <span class="search-hit mathjax">LLMs</span> across six prompting strategies. Results show that all models achieve, on average, an F1 score of 0.8 for code up to 200 NLOC. Our findings show that a single illustrative example enables the smallest model (8B) to match the performance of a much larger 70B model. In contrast, structured <span class="search-hit mathjax">reasoning</span> via Chain-of-Thought offers only marginal gains, primarily for larger models.
        <a class="is-size-7" style="white-space: nowrap;" onclick="document.getElementById('2509.19587v1-abstract-full').style.display = 'none'; document.getElementById('2509.19587v1-abstract-short').style.display = 'inline';">&#9651; Less</a>
      </span>
    </p>
    

    <p class="is-size-7"><span class="has-text-black-bis has-text-weight-semibold">Submitted</span> 23 September, 2025; 
      <span class="has-text-black-bis has-text-weight-semibold">originally announced</span> September 2025.
      
    </p>
    

    

    
  </li>

  <li class="arxiv-result">
    <div class="is-marginless">
      <p class="list-title is-inline-block"><a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.19571">arXiv:2509.19571</a>
        <span>&nbsp;[<a href="https://arxiv.org/pdf/2509.19571">pdf</a>, <a href="https://arxiv.org/ps/2509.19571">ps</a>, <a href="https://arxiv.org/format/2509.19571">other</a>]&nbsp;</span>
      </p>
      <div class="tags is-inline-block">
        <span class="tag is-small is-link tooltip is-tooltip-top" data-tooltip="Robotics">cs.RO</span>
        
          
